If the thing you eat is the most recent thing you ate (such as when
you wake up and immediately eat that partially-eaten food ration), the
code completely bypasses the checks for the effect of rottenness.  (It
also bypasses the food poisoning, acidic, poisonous, and mildly
sickening effects.)

If you eat anything else in between, you will be *guaranteed* to get
the "Blecch!" message and a chance at a negative effect.

I believe this is a bug, as when food is found to be rotten (by random
chance, see below) it is marked as orotten so that it will always be
found to be rotten in the future.

Here's more information than you asked for, since I've been digging in
the source:

First, food will be rotten (in the "Blecch!  Rotten..." sense) under
the following conditions:

* Corpses with a 1/7 chance, or any corpse previously found to be
  rotten.  Lizards, lichens, and corpses which sicken, attempt to
  poison, acid burn, or mildly sicken you are excluded.
* Other food items which are older than 30 [turns?] (50 if blessed)
  with a 1/7 chance, which have been previously found to be rotten, or
  which are cursed.  Fortune cookies are excluded [preservatives?].
* Non-food items which are cursed.

If rotten, the following occur:

* Get the "Blecch!" message.
* 1/4 chance you are confused for 2d4 turns
* otherwise 1/4 chance you are blinded for 2d10 turns
* otherwise 1/3 chance you lose consciousness for 1-10 turns.

(Then the nutrition in the food is halved for a food item or quartered
for a corpse [for a corpse only if you had an effect above].  If you
had an effect, you don't actually eat any of it.  If not, you continue
eating at the reduced nutrition.)


